<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Burdur</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Burdur is a city in the Lakes Region of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, near Lake Burdur. In the 14th century it belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a> of the Hamidids before passing to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> later in that century. It is now the capital of Burdur province, and the nearby ruins of Sagalassos are among the major ancient sites of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/burdur.jpg" alt="Burdur" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nihat1988 · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burdur_manzara_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Burdur stands on the shore of Lake Burdur in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. Habitation in the surrounding province goes back to 6500 BCE, and the earliest sign of settlement in the city itself dates to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-bronze-age">Early Bronze Age</a>. In antiquity the area belonged to the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisidia">Pisidia</a>. The city may have changed its location several times: the ancient town of Limnombria lay closer to the lake, and in the Byzantine period the city existed as Polydorion, from which the present name derives.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The urban history of Burdur is generally held to begin with the Turkish settlement that followed the Seljuq victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert">Manzikert</a> in 1071. Turks became the majority of the population after 1211, and the first settlement was moved uphill away from the lake because of malaria. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilij-arslan-ii">Kilij Arslan II</a>, the Seljuq sultan of Rum, imposed his rule in 1177, and the town was captured by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> in 1260. Trade through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antalya">Antalya</a> made it a commercial stop for tragacanth, wine, wax, wood and tar.</p><p>In 1300 the Hamidids under Dündar Bey took Burdur and built its Grand Mosque, around which the town developed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> captured it in 1324, and Dündar Bey's son recovered it in 1328. The traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> visited in 1330 and described a town of orchards and farms with a castle near the mosque and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> of ahis controlling commerce and production. The Ottoman sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Bayezid I</a> took Burdur in 1391, and in 1478 it had four quarters, three Muslim and one Christian.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>A small Greek population lived in the city until the exchange of populations between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, and an abandoned Greek church there has been turned into a museum with a large dinosaur fossil. Burdur has about 95 thousand inhabitants and is home to Mehmet Akif Ersoy University.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Burdur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Hamidids ruled the lake district around Egirdir, Isparta, <b>Burdur</b> and Uluborlu, a land of orchards and pastures on the routes between Konya and the coast.</blockquote><a href="/state/hamidids">Hamidids</a></li></ul></section>
